Multiple Inheritance ambiguity – Resolving Function Name Conflict

📌 Description

This C++ program demonstrates Multiple Inheritance and how to resolve a function name conflict when two base classes have functions with the same name.

Here:

- "Academic" → One base class
- "Sports" → Another base class
- "Student" → Derived class inheriting from both

Both "Academic" and "Sports" contain a function named "display()".

---

📊 Class Structure

Academic       Sports
    \           /
     \         /
       Student

---

📚 Classes Used

1. "Academic" Class

The "Academic" class contains a public "display()" function.

void display() const {
    std::cout << "Academic information\n";
}

This function displays academic information.

---

2. "Sports" Class

The "Sports" class also contains a "display()" function.

void display() const {
    std::cout << "Sports information\n";
}

This function displays sports information.

---

3. "Student" Class

The "Student" class inherits from both "Academic" and "Sports".

Since both base classes have a function named "display()", calling "display()" directly using a "Student" object creates ambiguity.

To resolve this ambiguity, the scope resolution operator "::" is used.

Academic::display();
Sports::display();

This explicitly tells the compiler which base class function should be called.

The "displayAll()" function calls both base class functions.

---

⚙️ Main Function

A "Student" object is created:

Student student;

The following calls explicitly select the required "display()" function:

student.Academic::display();
student.Sports::display();

Then:

student.displayAll();

calls both functions through the "Student" class.

---

🖥️ Output

Academic information
Sports information
Academic information
Sports information

